            %em the
            theta function one usually means the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacobi+theta+function"} Jacobi theta function
            (see there for more) for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi z
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z = 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
            as a function in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi τ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau
            the “theta function” is not actually a section of a line bundle anymore. The generalization in number theory of the Jacobi theta function that does again have a dependence on a twisting is the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirichlet+theta+function"} Dirichlet theta function
            depending on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirichlet+character"} Dirichlet character
            (which by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Artin+reciprocity"} Artin reciprocity
            corresponds to a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Galois+representation"} Galois representation
